    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Agility Bandits was created as a responsive blog for a fictional
        dog-agility training centre. Visitors needed a lively but readable way
        to discover articles, while an authenticated administrator needed clear
        paths for creating, editing, and managing posts. The design also had to
        remain stable once real API content replaced the mock content.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The brand needed to express the speed and playfulness of dog agility
            without making an editorial interface noisy. Variable titles,
            images, excerpts, and article lengths also meant the layout could
            not depend on perfectly controlled content.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Create energy through brand details while protecting reading
              comfort</li>
            <li>Design flexible cards and page sections for unpredictable API
              content</li>
            <li>Separate public reading journeys from authenticated admin tasks</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-agilitybanditsmoodboard-600.webp"
            data-src="design-agilitybanditsmoodboard-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="517"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Agility Bandits mood board showing energetic colours, typography, dogs, and sport-inspired graphics"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Mood board translating agility,
            movement, and approachability into a visual direction</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Combined strong colour, motion-inspired graphics, and playful
              brand elements with a simple editorial grid and readable
              typography
            </li>
            <li>
              Designed content cards to tolerate different title lengths, image
              proportions, and excerpt sizes without losing visual order
            </li>
            <li>
              Mapped public flows from discovery to reading separately from
              admin login, creation, editing, and deletion tasks
            </li>
            <li>
              Specified visible hover, focus, validation, and navigation states
              so interaction cues remain clear across mouse and keyboard use
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-agilitybandits-600.webp"
            data-src="design-agilitybandits-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="495"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Agility Bandits desktop wireframes and high-fidelity blog layouts in Figma"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Responsive blog structure for
            discovery, reading, and publishing tasks</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final design connects a distinctive sport-inspired identity with a
        practical content system that can accommodate real API data. It
        demonstrates an end-to-end design approach: brand direction, content
        hierarchy, responsive layouts, interaction states, and separate public
        and administrative journeys all support the same product rather than
        competing with one another.
      </p>
    </div>

